{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## 477b closed; S6's close blocked by an undeclared-file check that was right\n\n477b (toolchain fast-path race) CLOSED. Tonight's closes now: 3a02, 9a17, c379, 740d, 477b. Nine\nchanges merged: 2593, 2596, 2600, 2614, 2594, 2607, 2601, 2609, 2613.\n\nS6 (09da) CLOSE WAS BLOCKED and the refusal is worth recording as a good one. The merged commit\n76c994fc97ba touches five files the recorded file_impact never declared: _cli/_llm_commands.py,\n_config_resolvers.py, _errors.py, llm/errors.py and llm/gate_admission.py. Every one is defensible --\nthe new module, the two exception types, the error codes, the config resolver, and the latent\n'return 1' bug the implementation found -- but defensible is not declared, and the gate measures\nwhether what SHIPPED matches what was REVIEWED.\n\nTHE MOST INSTRUCTIVE OMISSION IS gate_admission.py ITSELF, the new module at the centre of the change.\nThe reviewed plan named the seams it would wrap (plan_review/__init__.py, completion.py,\n_mcp_llm.py), the config paths and the test file -- but never named the module that would do the\nwrapping. That is easy to do and hard to catch on a read-through: the plan reads COMPLETE because\nevery file it mentions is real. The gap is a file that is ABSENT, not a claim that is wrong. Same\nshape as tonight's recurring finding -- absence is what does not announce itself -- applied to a plan\nrather than a test run.\n\nWidened file_impact to eleven paths with per-path reasons and re-ran review-plan. Kept _mcp_llm.py\neven though it ultimately needed NO edit -- making GateCongestedError an LLMError subclass with a\nretryable outcome routed it through arms that already existed, so the design SHRANK under review --\nbecause dropping it silently would misrepresent what was reviewed.\n\nDid not use --force. The gate is correct that the shipped work exceeded the reviewed plan; forcing\npast it would defeat exactly the check that just did its job, and agents are barred from it anyway.\nThe cost is one 15-20 minute re-review, which is the right price for the property being protected."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788522509866775001,"uuid":"af9c872f-d883-4e6e-90d3-693ef54be25c"}